<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Scribonius Curio (tribune 50 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 84–<a href="/years/49-bce">49 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Scribonius Curio was a Roman politician who, as tribune of the plebs in 50 BCE, defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s interests in the Senate in the months before the civil war. At its outbreak Caesar sent him to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In August 49 BCE he was drawn into an ambush by the forces of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> River and died fighting with his army.</p></div>
